# 2026-09-19, #438 step 3: resolve_trait IS the name resolver — the one place a
# written trait name is turned into an identity, in the scope the name was
# written in (current package, then imports, then the prelude). Its argument is
# a spelling BY DEFINITION, as find_struct_by_name's is; what it returns is the
# identity everything downstream uses. The row is here so the count cannot grow
# unnoticed: a second resolver would be the defect.
#ARGSCAN resolve_trait 1 bd8ed301
# 2026-09-19, #438 step 4: the impl registry's three entry points. Each takes a
# trait spelling — a path, a name written in the scope the call is in, or a name
# the COMPILER spells itself ("Drop", "Copy", the auto markers) — and returns
# the one identity the registry is keyed by (`impl_trait_id`: path, then scope,
# then the lang-item table, then the single trait of that name; ambiguity
# answers nothing). A spelled argument here is the POINT: this is where a
# spelling stops being one. The counts are pinned so a fourth entry point, or a
# site that goes around them back to a raw key, shows up as a diff.
# 2026-09-28: has_impl 17 -> 19, roster a2e2eb9a -> 26a90fb6; impl_key 5 -> 7, roster cbc0ca3c -> 37c1f856.
# lower_place_assign asks whether an indexed receiver is an `IndexMut` struct (the written element is
# then a live `&mut` referent), and index_output_type_ reads `Index`'s Output off the impl — the same
# trait spellings over the same concrete/base struct names try_index_mut_assign already passes.
